<header class="sg-head"><p class="featured-region">Lament · c. 1210 · Europe</p><h1 class="sg-title">O Fortuna</h1><p class="sg-kid" data-say>A Latin poem from about 800 years ago about Fortune, who spins a wheel that lifts people up and throws them down.</p></header>
<dl class="sg-facts"><div><dt>Words</dt><dd>anonymous</dd></div><div><dt>Culture</dt><dd>Goliards, the Latin poets of the Carmina Burana</dd></div><div><dt>Language</dt><dd>Latin</dd></div><div><dt>Place</dt><dd>not known (preserved in the Benediktbeuern manuscript of the Carmina Burana)</dd></div><div><dt>Date</dt><dd>early 13th century</dd></div><div><dt>Type</dt><dd>Lament</dd></div><div><dt>The words</dt><dd>Public domain (anonymous Goliardic poem, early 13th century)</dd></div></dl>
<div class="sg-body">
<section class="sg-sec" id="story"><h2>The story</h2><p>&quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/o-fortuna">O Fortuna</a>&quot; is a medieval <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> Goliardic poem of uncertain authorship, written in the early 13th century. It belongs to the collection known as the Carmina Burana, preserved in the Benediktbeuern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscript</a>. The poem is a complaint against Fortuna, in Roman mythology the inexorable fate that rules both gods and mortals.</p><p>Its subject belongs with a familiar medieval image, the Rota Fortunae or Wheel of Fortune, which the goddess spins at random so that some people suffer while others find wealth; the wheel appears in the version of the collection known as the Codex Buranus. Writing in Harper's, Scott Horton observed that the text shows how few people of its time &quot;felt any control over their own destiny&quot;, while it also &quot;rings with a passion for life&quot;.</p><p>The poem reached a wide modern audience through a later work. In 1934 the German composer Carl Orff came across the collection and, with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> and Greek enthusiast Michel Hofmann, chose and arranged 24 of its poems into a libretto. His cantata Carmina Burana, composed in 1935–1936 and first staged by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankfurt">Frankfurt</a> Opera on 8 June 1937, opens and closes with &quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/o-fortuna">O Fortuna</a>&quot; in the sections titled &quot;Fortuna Imperatrix Mundi&quot; (&quot;Fortune, Empress of the World&quot;). Orff's setting is a separate, much later work that is still in copyright.</p><p>Through that setting the medieval words became one of the most widely heard pieces of classical music. In 2009 it topped a BBC list of the most widely heard classical tracks of the previous 75 years in the United Kingdom. Its use in film trailers, television and advertising, from the trailer of Excalibur (1981) onward, has led writers such as Horton and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/david">David</a> Clem to note how far the music has been detached from the poem's subject of fate and human struggle.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="meaning"><h2>What it says</h2><p>The poem is a complaint addressed to Fortune, the fate that rules gods and mortals alike and, like the turning Wheel of Fortune, brings some people suffering and others wealth at random. Its mood is one of helplessness before fate, while still, as Horton put it, holding on to a passion for life.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="words"><h2>The words</h2><div class="sg-show" role="group" aria-label="Show these columns"><button type="button" aria-pressed="true" data-col="orig">Latin</button><button type="button" aria-pressed="true" data-col="en">In English</button></div><div class="sg-blocks"><div class="sg-lyrics sg-cols-2"><div class="sg-colhead" aria-hidden="true"><span class="sg-o">Latin</span><span class="sg-e">In English</span></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">O Fortuna</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>O Fortune,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">velut luna</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>like the moon</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">statu variabilis,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>changeable in state,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">semper crescis</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>you always wax</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">aut decrescis;</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>or wane;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">vita detestabilis</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>hateful life</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">nunc obdurat</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>now hardens</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">et tunc curat</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>and then soothes</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">ludo mentis aciem,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>the mind's keenness at its sport;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">egestatem,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>poverty,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">potestatem</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>power,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">dissolvit ut glaciem.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>it melts them like ice.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">Sors immanis</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Fate, monstrous</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">et inanis,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>and empty,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">rota tu volubilis,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>you turning wheel,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">status malus,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>a wicked state,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">vana salus</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>a vain well-being,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">semper dissolubilis,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>always ready to dissolve,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">obumbrata</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>overshadowed</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">et velata</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>and veiled,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">michi quoque niteris;</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>you bear down on me too;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">nunc per ludum</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>now through your game</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">dorsum nudum</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>my bare back</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">fero tui sceleris.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>I bring to your villainy.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">Sors salutis</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>The lot of health</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">et virtutis</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>and of virtue</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">michi nunc contraria,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>is now against me;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">est affectus</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>driven on</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">et defectus</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>and worn down,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">semper in angaria.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>always in forced service.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">Hac in hora</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>In this hour,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">sine mora</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>without delay,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">corde pulsum tangite;</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>touch the plucked string;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">quod per sortem</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>since by chance</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">sternit fortem,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>she lays low the strong man,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">mecum omnes plangite!</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>all weep with me!</span></p></div></div></div><div class="sg-credit"><p>Original: Carmina Burana (early 13th century). Text from <a href="https://la.wikisource.org/wiki/Carmina_Burana_(Orff)%2FFortuna_Imperatrix_Mundi" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikisource</a>; public domain.</p><p>The English is the Institute&#8217;s own translation, made from the original for this page.</p></div><details class="sg-notes"><summary>Notes on the translation (8)</summary><ul><li>line 1.9: 'ludo mentis aciem' is read as the object of 'obdurat' and 'curat': life hardens and then soothes the mind's sharpness, as in a game. The construction is loose and readings differ.</li><li>line 1.12: the subject of 'dissolvit' is life (or Fortune); it melts poverty and power alike.</li><li>line 2.4: 'status malus' may be read as an address to Fortune ('[you are] a wicked state') or as the condition she brings.</li><li>line 2.12: 'dorsum nudum fero' = 'I offer my bare back' (to be flogged), i.e. I am at the mercy of your wickedness; also read 'I carry my bare back' (stripped by Fortune's game).</li><li>lines 3.4-3.5: 'affectus et defectus' is disputed: the will and its failing, or 'driven on and worn down'; here taken as the speaker's state.</li><li>line 3.6: 'angaria' is forced labour, compulsory service.</li><li>line 3.9: 'corde pulsum tangite' plays on 'corda/chorda' (string) and 'cor' (heart): 'strike the plucked string' (play the lament) or 'touch the throbbing heart'.</li><li>line 3.11: the subject of 'sternit' is Fortune (or fate): 'she lays low the strong'.</li></ul></details></section>
</div>
